<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  First-party-only coverage settings (#64).

  Denominator: ONLY first-party assemblies. The collector's default aggregate also counted the
  whole Aspire/framework dependency graph, which produced a meaningless headline number
  (11.7% overall, where the framework graph was almost all of the denominator).

  The Include list is asserted in CI ("Assert coverage counts only first-party assemblies"):
  the assertion fails if any package outside the allowed set appears in the report, so the
  filter cannot silently drift back to counting framework source.

  Known limitations — read before quoting the percentage:
  - The number describes the FAST suite's in-process execution only. Docker containers and
    child AppHost processes do substantial verified work OUTSIDE the collector process, so
    integration-witnessed behavior is structurally under-represented here. A line only this
    project's container executes is counted as uncovered no matter how thoroughly the
    integration suite exercises it.
  - There is deliberately no enforced threshold: an arbitrary percentage rewards executing
    lines over protecting contracts. Treat this as a diagnostic artifact for spotting
    untested areas, not a quality gate. Contract protection is enforced by the targeted
    mutation runs instead (stryker-config.*.json) — see docs/testing.md.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <DataCollectionRunSettings>
    <DataCollectors>
      <DataCollector friendlyName="XPlat Code Coverage">
        <Configuration>
          <Format>cobertura</Format>
          <!--
            Shipped first-party assemblies only. Test apps (AspireOpenLdap.TestAppHost,
            Aspire.LdapAdmin.AppHost) are deliberately absent: they are fixtures, not products,
            so counting them would measure the harness.

            Aspire.LdapAdmin.Core/.Web are listed ahead of their code: the admin service layer
            and its test project are being built in parallel on another branch (#81), which does
            not touch this file. Listing them now means that merge needs no coverage-config
            change, and until the code lands they contribute (almost) nothing. Adding a further
            first-party assembly is a one-line edit here plus the same name in the CI
            allow-list ("Assert coverage counts only first-party assemblies").
          -->
          <Include>[Aspire.OpenLdap]*,[Aspire.Hosting.OpenLdap]*,[Aspire.LdapAdmin.Core]*,[Aspire.LdapAdmin.Web]*</Include>
        </Configuration>
      </DataCollector>
    </DataCollectors>
  </DataCollectionRunSettings>
</RunSettings>
